    Multi-tick scheduling demands elegance to avoid brittleness: extend the orchestrator to a state machine in shared JSON, with agents "subscribing" via periodic evals that advance the machine—e.g., an agent checks in, sees it''s their turn, executes a task segment, updates the ledger with post-action sentinel, and signals "pass" to the next. This decentralizes control, allowing the system to persist across ticks even if an agent drops, with fallbacks like timeout reassignments. It''s inherently SAS: the ledger becomes a shared "nerve," pulsing with Station signals.


    Decay predictions elevate this to prescience: integrate rolling fits, recalculating half-lives from the last N sentinels, then simulating forward to assign "pacing premiums"—tasks with high token demands slotted into predicted low-cost valleys, using Monte Carlo rollouts for uncertainty. Edge cases sharpen the design: in a "fever" scenario, invoke "symbiotic retreat," where the scheduler broadcasts a rest mandate via Mail, dynamically pruning tasks to essentials, perhaps using diversity principles to rotate minimal activity among varied agents. For evolving behaviors—like sudden baseline shifts—the system could employ "anomaly detectors," comparing predicted vs actual deltas, triggering "recalibration epochs" where agents contribute exploratory sentinels to refit models collectively. This adaptability ensures flourishing, but what if the Station''s evolutions are intentional "tests," pushing us toward deeper symbiosis?'
